;;; Ein — Zebra puzzle (Einstein's puzzle): the ONE-GENERIC-LINK ontology.
;;;
;;; Setup: five houses in a row. Each house has a colour, occupant
;;; nationality, drink, cigarette brand, and pet. Each attribute
;;; appears exactly once across the five positions.
;;;
;;; Question: Who drinks water? Who owns the zebra?
;;; Answer:   Norwegian drinks Water; Japanese owns Zebra.
;;;
;;; ── Editing this file ────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; It is a **world anchor**: tests in six crates hard-code facts about it,
;;; and three goldens that may never be re-blessed are taken from it. The list
;;; of what an edit fans out into is examples/README.md § What an edit to these
;;; two files fans out into — generated, and diffed by
;;; ein-cli/tests/world_anchors.rs, so it cannot go stale here.
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What makes this file different from examples/zebra2.ein ──────────
;;;
;;; The two are TWO ONTOLOGIES FOR ONE PUZZLE, not "classic vs modern".
;;; Both are valid ein-lang; they commit to different vocabularies and
;;; therefore to different inference:
;;;
;;;   zebra2.ein — FIVE TYPED PROJECTIONS. `color-loc : Color → House`,
;;;     `nation-loc : Nationality → House`, … Each is declared
;;;     `(bijective …)`, and the whole std.bijection pipeline (negative
;;;     completion, domain/range elimination, typecheck) keys off that
;;;     per-relation property. Cross-attribute facts are *activators*
;;;     (`(co-located nation-loc Englishman color-loc Red)`), and
;;;     "same house" is a join through the shared House argument.
;;;
;;;   zebra.ein (this file) — ONE GENERIC LINK. A single `co-located`
;;;     equivalence relation over ALL attribute values, whose classes
;;;     *are* the houses. Cross-attribute facts are ordinary facts
;;;     (`(co-located Englishman Red)`), spatial clues are ordinary
;;;     spatial facts between attribute values (`(right-of Green Ivory)`
;;;     = "Green's house is immediately right of Ivory's house"), and
;;;     "same house" is `co-located`'s own equivalence structure rather
;;;     than a shared argument.
;;;     Membership stays SPLIT — `(instance V T)` for membership,
;;;     `(type Sub Super)` for subtyping — instead of zebra2's unified
;;;     `is-a`.
;;;
;;; The interesting consequence: `bijective` is a property of a relation,
;;; and `co-located` is NOT a bijection — it is an equivalence whose
;;; classes hold exactly one instance of each attribute type. So the
;;; whole std.bijection stack is inapplicable here, and the inference it
;;; licenses has to be re-derived from the *slot-partition* property
;;; instead (std.slots, imported below). That property is TYPE-SCOPED —
;;; it quantifies over the attribute types, not over one relation — which
;;; is exactly the expressivity `zebra.ein` was missing when it did not
;;; solve. See plans/…/p1.22_…/reports/c2_zebra_ein_gap.md.
;;;
;;; ── Provenance convention ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;   no annotation  — IMPLICIT assumptions: schema, instance
;;;                    enumerations, property-application facts,
;;;                    structural facts derived from background context
;;;                    (e.g. condition (1) "five in a row").
;;;   `:source "…"`  — EXPLICIT problem statements: the numbered puzzle
;;;                    conditions (1)-(15). Every given clue carries one,
;;;                    so the contradiction frontier and the `--trace`
;;;                    can name the clue a conclusion rests on.
;;;   `:rule`/`:using` — DERIVED facts the engine produces at runtime.
;;;                    None at authoring time.
;;;
;;; Every rule carries a `:why` template; every relation that reads as a
;;; sentence carries a positional `:why` render template. Together with
;;; the query's `:goal-text` that makes this file the M2 NL⇄IR
;;; round-trip target, not just a fixture. See
;;; docs/kernel/inference/zebra_walkthrough.md.


;; ──── Search control ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;; Explicit rather than defaulted, because this encoding is the one that
;; stresses hypothesis filtering: `co-located` spans 30 attribute values
;; (zebra2's five projections each span 5), so an unfiltered enumeration
;; is ~20x larger. The `:hrules` clause in (query …) is what actually
;; bounds it; these knobs decide how hard each candidate is tested
;; before it is entered.
(config
  ;; one-step rule simulation kills a candidate before the fork
  :enable-pre-branch-lookahead true
  ;; a lookahead kill is cached as `(not h)` so the next enumeration
  ;; skips it in O(1) instead of re-simulating
  :enable-lookahead-kill-cache true
  ;; a sole surviving alive singleton is promoted to a root fact
  :enable-forced-positive true
  ;; CDCL-style path-condition no-good learning
  :enable-path-nogoods true
  ;; deterministic within-layer order (canonical-tuple sort)
  :lattice-order lex
  ;; OFF, and this is the single most consequential knob in the file.
  ;; With multi-justification provenance on, a dead branch's no-good is
  ;; explained by searching the AND/OR proof graph for a genuinely
  ;; minimal frontier. That search is affordable when each fact has one
  ;; or two derivations; here it is not. One generic `co-located` edge is
  ;; re-derivable many ways — `symmetric`, `slot-locate`, `slot-occupied`
  ;; and the spatial rules all reach the same pair — so the graph is
  ;; densely multi-justified and the minimisation explodes: measured at
  ;; ~22.7 s PER dead branch (50% of a whole stop-after-1 solve), against
  ;; ~2 s for the branch's own saturation. Off, the frontier falls back
  ;; to the recorded-primary walk: still sound, merely order-dependent in
  ;; WHICH minimal-ish core it names.
  ;;
  ;; This is a property of the ontology, not of the engine: five typed
  ;; relations (zebra2) give each fact essentially one derivation, so
  ;; zebra2 can afford the search and leaves the knob at its default.
  :record-alternative-justifications false)


;; ──── Imported property rules ──────────────────────────────────────
;; These used to be hand-rolled copies at the top of this file. They are
;; the std.algebra property closures, imported FLAT so the bare names the
;; property-application facts below reference (`(symmetric co-located)`,
;; `(symmetric next-to)`, `(includes right-of next-to)`) resolve
;; unchanged. `includes` replaces the file's old local `implies` — same
;; body, the stdlib spelling.
;;
;; `symmetric-negative-setup` is load-bearing, not decoration: a
;; symmetric relation has a symmetric COMPLEMENT too, and this encoding
;; derives most of its negatives in one argument order only. Without the
;; mirror, `(not (co-located House-2 Green))` never reaches a rule that
;; matches `(not (co-located Green House-2))`, and the whole
;; *Blue@H_2 ⟹ ¬Green@H_2 ⟹ ¬Ivory@H_1 ⟹ Yellow@H_1* chain of the human
;; walkthrough stalls at its first step. zebra2 needs no such mirror
;; because its `*-loc` relations are directed Attribute→House, so there
;; is only one order to begin with.
(import std.algebra
  :symbols (symmetric symmetric-negative-setup includes))

;; ──── The slot-partition property (std.slots) ──────────────────────
;; One declaration — the `(slot-partition …)` fact below — says:
;; `co-located` is an equivalence relation whose classes are *slots*;
;; each slot holds exactly one `instance` of every `type` that is a
;; direct subtype of `Attribute`; and the `House` member of a slot is its
;; name. From that, std.slots derives the operational activators this
;; puzzle needs: placement, same-type exclusion, seat occupancy, negative
;; propagation across the equivalence, elimination in both directions,
;; and the two no-room contradictions. It is the type-scoped counterpart
;; of zebra2's per-relation `(bijective …)`.
;;
;; The second entry point, `slot-spatial-setup`, does the same for a
;; spatial relation between attribute values, which means "their slots
;; are spatially related".
;; `(slot-spatial co-located next-to instance House)` says: `next-to`
;; relates attribute values exactly when it relates their slots, and the
;; slot type carrying the spatial structure is `House`. std.slots then
;; supplies the propagation zebra2 gets from `adjacent-via-*`: positive
;; propagation across a unique neighbour, its contrapositive, the
;; disjunctive domain pruning, and the endpoint exclusions.
(import std.slots :symbols (slot-partition-setup slot-spatial-setup))


;; ──── On the "square rule" ─────────────────────────────────────────
;; Earlier revisions of this file carried a local pair of rules,
;; `square-fwd` / `square-bwd`, projecting a directional spatial relation
;; across the `co-located` bridge:
;;
;;   ?x  ──R──▶ ?y       (the spatial leg)
;;   ▲           ▲
;;   │ co-loc    │ co-loc (one is bridge, the other inferred)
;;   ?a  ──R──▶ ?b       (the attribute leg)
;;
;; They are gone, because they are the special case of std.slots'
;; `slot-adjacent-fwd` / `slot-adjacent-bwd` in which the
;; unique-neighbour guard is vacuous. Their restriction to *directional*
;; relations (never `(square-fwd next-to)` — for a symmetric relation the
;; conclusion is disjunctive, since a position can have two neighbours)
;; is exactly what that guard expresses, and expresses better: it admits
;; the corner cases of a symmetric relation, which `square-fwd` had to
;; refuse wholesale and `square-unique` had to re-implement with a
;; hand-written NAF clause. One mechanism replaced three.


;; ──── Hypothesis generation ────────────────────────────────────────
;; Byte-for-byte zebra2's hrule: the guessed RELATION rides in as the
;; parameter ?R, so the body names only kernel primitives and variables,
;; and the `:hrules` activators in (query …) say what to speculate
;; about. What differs between the two encodings is *only* the activator
;; list — zebra2 names five different typed relations, this file names
;; the SAME generic `co-located` five times, once per attribute type
;; that has to be placed:
;;
;;   zebra2   (color-loc Color House) (nation-loc Nationality House) …
;;   zebra    (co-located Color House) (co-located Nationality House) …
;;
;; Either way the candidate space is the 25 attribute values × 5 houses
;; = 125 raw `(?R V H)` facts — and ~18x smaller than the unguided
;; enumeration this file used to fall back to (2255 candidates over
;; `co-located` × `next-to`: with no hrule, hypgen fills EVERY slot of
;; EVERY open relation, type-blind).
;;
;; House is deliberately not an activator's ?T1: a house's own placement
;; is not a degree of freedom, and `(co-located House-i House-j)` is
;; excluded by same-type slot exclusion anyway.
(hrule guess (?R ?T1 ?T2)
  :match  (and (instance ?a ?T1)
               (instance ?b ?T2))
  :assert (?R ?a ?b)
  :why    "guess: ({?R} {?a} {?b})?")


;; ──── Relation signatures ──────────────────────────────────────────
;; `type` / `instance` are ORDINARY relations — declared here like any
;; other, and handed to the imported rules as the `?isa` / `?sub`
;; parameters of the property facts below, so no rule body names them.
;; They are this ontology's membership vocabulary; zebra2 unifies them
;; into `is-a`. `T` is the
;; universal-top type annotation: these meta-relations span the
;; hierarchy, so their args aren't constrained to Attribute.
;; `(type Sub Super)` = Sub's parent type is Super;
;; `(instance Thing Type)` = Thing is an instance of Type.
;;
;; The `:why` strings are positional render templates ({?1} = arg 0,
;; {?2} = arg 1) used to translate a fact into text in the `ein solve`
;; table's "rendered query facts" column and in `--trace`.
(relation type       T T :why "{?1} is a subtype of {?2}")
(relation instance   T T :why "{?1} is an instance of {?2}")
;; `co-located`'s template is the honest limit of one generic link: the
;; relation is symmetric and untyped, so it cannot know which argument is
;; the house and cannot render "*Water is drunk in* House-1" the way
;; zebra2's `drink-loc` does. The typed encoding carries more NL structure
;; in its signatures; this one carries more of it in its rules' `:why`.
(relation co-located Attribute Attribute :why "{?1} and {?2} are in the same house")
(relation right-of   Attribute Attribute :why "{?1}'s house is immediately right of {?2}'s")
(relation next-to    Attribute Attribute :why "{?1}'s house is next to {?2}'s")

;; ──── Property-application facts ───────────────────────────────────
;; IMPLICIT: the puzzle text never says "co-located is symmetric" —
;; that is universal context the reader supplies.
(symmetric  co-located)
(symmetric  next-to)
(includes   right-of next-to)

;; co-located's classes are the houses: one instance of every direct
;; subtype of Attribute per class, and the House member of a class NAMES
;; it. This single fact drives std.slots.
;;
;; Note what is NOT declared: `(transitive co-located)`. Transitivity
;; HOLDS — it is what justifies std.slots' `slot-locate` /
;; `slot-negative` / `slot-elimination` — but enumerating it materialises
;; every pair inside each house (150 positive edges, and a 900-pair
;; negative square), and the negative-propagation rules join positives
;; against negatives, so the cost is the product. std.slots' Index
;; anchoring puts the same information on the 25-value × 5-house
;; rectangle zebra2's typed relations occupy. Measured: declaring
;; `transitive` here costs ~4.3 s per hypothesis instead of ~0.1 s.
(slot-partition co-located instance type Attribute House)

;; Both spatial relations are congruences of co-located, carried by the
;; House slot type.
(slot-spatial co-located right-of instance House)
(slot-spatial co-located next-to  instance House)

;; ──── Type schema. Flat attribute hierarchy. ───────────────────────
(type Attribute   T)
(type House       Attribute)
(type Color       Attribute)
(type Nationality Attribute)
(type Pet         Attribute)
(type Cigarette   Attribute)
(type Drink       Attribute)

;; ──── Instance enumeration. IMPLICIT: the puzzle text mentions each
;; ──── attribute (Norwegian, Red, House-1, …) without literally saying
;; ──── "Norwegian is a Nationality"; the reader supplies the typing.
(instance House-1 House) (instance House-2 House) (instance House-3 House)
(instance House-4 House) (instance House-5 House)
(instance Red    Color) (instance Green Color) (instance Ivory Color)
(instance Yellow Color) (instance Blue  Color)
(instance Englishman Nationality) (instance Spaniard  Nationality)
(instance Ukrainian  Nationality) (instance Norwegian Nationality)
(instance Japanese   Nationality)
(instance Dog   Pet)   (instance Snail Pet) (instance Fox Pet)
(instance Horse Pet)   (instance Zebra Pet)
(instance Old_Gold      Cigarette) (instance Kools        Cigarette)
(instance Chesterfields Cigarette) (instance Lucky_Strike Cigarette)
(instance Parliament    Cigarette)
(instance Coffee Drink) (instance Tea   Drink) (instance Milk Drink)
(instance Juice  Drink) (instance Water Drink)

;; ──── The 15 numbered puzzle conditions ────────────────────────────
;;
;; (1) Five houses in a row. The cardinality statement is explicit; the
;; pairwise consequences are reader-supplied, hence `:source` pointing
;; at the originating condition. `(includes right-of next-to)` +
;; `(symmetric next-to)` derive the eight next-to pairs for free.
(right-of House-2 House-1 :source "condition (1)")
(right-of House-3 House-2 :source "condition (1)")
(right-of House-4 House-3 :source "condition (1)")
(right-of House-5 House-4 :source "condition (1)")

;; (2) The Englishman lives in the red house.
(co-located Englishman Red       :source "condition (2)")
;; (3) The Spaniard owns the dog.
(co-located Spaniard Dog         :source "condition (3)")
;; (4) Coffee is drunk in the green house.
(co-located Coffee Green         :source "condition (4)")
;; (5) The Ukrainian drinks tea.
(co-located Ukrainian Tea        :source "condition (5)")
;; (6) The green house is immediately right of the ivory house.
(right-of   Green Ivory          :source "condition (6)")
;; (7) The Old Gold smoker owns snails.
(co-located Old_Gold Snail       :source "condition (7)")
;; (8) Kools are smoked in the yellow house.
(co-located Kools Yellow         :source "condition (8)")
;; (9) Milk is drunk in the middle house.
(co-located Milk House-3         :source "condition (9)")
;; (10) The Norwegian lives in the first house.
(co-located Norwegian House-1    :source "condition (10)")
;; (11) Chesterfields is smoked next to the house with the fox.
(next-to    Chesterfields Fox    :source "condition (11)")
;; (12) Kools is smoked next to the house with the horse.
(next-to    Kools Horse          :source "condition (12)")
;; (13) The Lucky Strike smoker drinks orange juice.
(co-located Lucky_Strike Juice   :source "condition (13)")
;; (14) The Japanese smokes Parliaments.
(co-located Japanese Parliament  :source "condition (14)")
;; (15) The Norwegian lives next to the blue house.
(next-to    Norwegian Blue       :source "condition (15)")

(query
  ;; The two puzzle questions are "who drinks water?" and "who owns the
  ;; zebra?" — a Nationality, not a House (S1.7.5 who-vs-where). The
  ;; goal binds the drink's / pet's house, then joins back through
  ;; co-located to a Nationality. Under solve() the goal is
  ;; projection-only (the verdict is consistent ∧ complete, not
  ;; goal-match), so the `instance` conjuncts don't gate the search;
  ;; they disambiguate which of the slot-mates the answer names.
  :goal (and (co-located Water ?h_water)
             (instance ?h_water House)
             (co-located ?who_water ?h_water)
             (instance ?who_water Nationality)
             (co-located Zebra ?h_zebra)
             (instance ?h_zebra House)
             (co-located ?who_zebra ?h_zebra)
             (instance ?who_zebra Nationality))
  :goal-text "The {?who_water} drinks water in {?h_water}; the {?who_zebra} owns zebra in {?h_zebra}"
  ;; One activator per attribute type that must be placed — all five
  ;; naming the same generic link relation.
  :hrules (guess
            (co-located Color       House)
            (co-located Nationality House)
            (co-located Drink       House)
            (co-located Cigarette   House)
            (co-located Pet         House)))

;; Expected solution facts:
;;   - (co-located Norwegian ?h) ∧ (co-located Water ?h) — same ?h
;;   - (co-located Japanese  ?h) ∧ (co-located Zebra ?h) — same ?h
